{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md | sed -n '1,82p;137,177p;202,243p;310,395p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm de Bruijn linker over dao.stream (Phase B6)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, revised; not implemented\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. Objective and architectural position\n     6\t\n     7\tThis document specifies the closed-image code linker for `yin.vm` over\n     8\t`dao.stream` (backed by `dao.jing` content addressing). It is Phase B6 of\n     9\tthe de Bruijn compilation and VM architecture.\n    10\t\n    11\tGoverning rule: **B6 is used by both the stack VM and the register VM for\n    12\tlinking code over `dao.stream`.**\n    13\t\n    14\tFoundational principle: **The linker fetches code over `dao.stream`, but the\n    15\tcode itself is stored in `dao.jing`.**\n    16\t\n    17\tIn datom.world, code and continuations flow through streams: one universal\n    18\tAST, multiple execution backends (stack VM and register VM), peer witnesses\n    19\tto the same truth. Executable code is content-addressed data stored in\n    20\t`dao.jing`, while linking, resolution, and fetching occur strictly over\n    21\tappend-only `dao.stream` boundaries. Upstream stages -- Yang, the resolver\n    22\t(B2), the stack lowerer (B2), and the register compiler (R1, R2) -- produce\n    23\tcontent-addressed executable images:\n    24\t- Stack images, identified by H (`:yin.debruijn.code`)\n    25\t- Register images, identified by R (`:yin.debruijn.register`)\n    26\t\n    27\tThe linker is the component that enables either VM to resolve, fetch,\n    28\tverify, and link those executable images over `dao.stream`. A host holding\n    29\tonly an identity (H or R) and an index can obtain the image over a stream,\n    30\tprove it matches the storage address, prove it matches the requested\n    31\tcontract identity, validate its structural and liveness invariants, verify\n    32\tfree-variable closure for the receiving environment, and deliver it for\n    33\texecution.\n    34\t\n    35\tPhase B6 is implemented alone as the dedicated linker phase. It establishes\n    36\tthe format-parameterized fetch function (`yin.vm.debruijn-linker/fetch`) and\n    37\tprovides the concrete format records for both the stack and register VMs.\n    38\t\n    39\t## 2. Invariants: storage in dao.jing, fetching over dao.stream\n    40\t\n    41\tThe linker embodies the separation between storage and stream transport:\n    42\t\n    43\t1. **Storage in `dao.jing`**: The executable code itself (the canonical\n    44\t   instruction vector or register image map) is stored as an immutable,\n    45\t   content-addressed value in `dao.jing`, located at its `segment-key`.\n    46\t2. **Fetching over `dao.stream`**: Linking is an append-only stream process.\n    47\t   When code is resolved, requested, or transferred across execution\n    48\t   contexts or remote nodes, all exchanges travel strictly over\n    49\t   `dao.stream`. No direct function calls cross process or host boundaries.\n    50\t3. **Reused DaoJing substrate**: The linker builds directly on DaoJing:\n    51\t   - `dao.jing.dht` handles local cache retrieval and Kademlia peer lookup.\n    52\t   - `dao.jing.remote` handles DHT requests over `dao.stream` using\n    53\t     `content-client` and `default-handlers`.\n    54\t\n    55\tB6 reuses this substrate wholesale. The linker writes no transport, no peer\n    56\tlookup, no cache, and no responder of its own.\n    57\t\n    58\t### 2.1 What B6 adds\n    59\t\n    60\tB6 contributes:\n    61\t\n    62\t1. The format record protocol (section 5).\n    63\t2. The format-neutral six-step fetch function (section 4).\n    64\t3. Stack format record (`:yin.debruijn.code`) for stack VM code.\n    65\t4. Register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`) for register VM code.\n    66\t5. The H index and R index mapping identities to Jing storage addresses.\n    67\t6. Same-root pairing (H and R) for cross-VM fallback over streams.\n    68\t7. The qualified refusal vocabulary (section 4.3).\n    69\t8. Tri-host tests exercising local and remote fetch across JVM, CLJS,\n    70\t   and ClojureDart.\n    71\t\n    72\t### 2.2 What B6 does not add\n    73\t\n    74\t- No new transport: `dao.stream` is the only medium; `dao.jing.remote`\n    75\t  provides the RPC client and server.\n    76\t- No peer routing or caching: handled entirely by DaoJing DHT.\n    77\t- No global registry: handles, indexes, and format records are explicit\n    78\t  arguments.\n    79\t- No callbacks: every outcome is a returned, qualified data value.\n    80\t- No execution coupling: the linker fetches and verifies; running the\n    81\t  image belongs to the VM kernels or lifting.\n    82\t\n   137\timages.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   140\t\n   141\tFetch executes six steps in strict order:\n   142\t\n   143\t```clojure\n   144\t;; (fetch handle index format identity)\n   145\t;; 1. address  <- (index identity)\n   146\t;;               absent entry            -> :absent\n   147\t;; 2. value    <- (jing/get handle address absent)\n   148\t;;               absent value            -> :absent\n   149\t;;               (not (jing/segment-matches? address value))\n   150\t;;                                       -> :address-mismatch\n   151\t;; 3.           (not= identity ((:hash-fn format) value))\n   152\t;;                                       -> :hash-mismatch\n   153\t;; 4.           ((:validate-fn format) value) returns a defect\n   154\t;;                                       -> that qualified defect\n   155\t;; 5.           ((:free-names-fn format) value) against receiver environment\n   156\t;;               unresolved name         -> :unresolved-free\n   157\t;;               name shadowed by store  -> :shadowed-free\n   158\t;; 6. return the verified image\n   159\t```\n   160\t\n   161\tStep order is load-bearing:\n   162\t\n   163\t1. Address verification (step 2) precedes identity verification (step 3)\n   164\t   because the storage address was requested from the store. Crucially,\n   165\t   `(jing/segment-matches? address value)` verifies the value against the\n   166\t   specific hash algorithm carried by `address`, rather than assuming\n   167\t   DaoJing's current minting default.\n   168\t2. Validation (step 4) precedes the closure check (step 5) so that\n   169\t   `:free-names-fn` only ever encounters structurally valid images. For\n   170\t   register images, step 4 includes the verified in-band live-set rules.\n   171\t3. The closure check (step 5) precedes return so that an unclosed image is\n   172\t   never handed to the caller. D11 requires refusal, not execution, for an\n   173\t   image the receiver cannot bind identically.\n   174\t\n   175\tThere is no separate descriptor check. The descriptor hash and contract\n   176\tversion are embedded inside H and R. A descriptor or version disagreement\n   177\tfails at step 3 as `:hash-mismatch`.\n   202\t## 5. Format records: stack and register\n   203\t\n   204\tB6 provides the format records for both execution backends:\n   205\t\n   206\t### 5.1 Stack format record (`:yin.debruijn.code`)\n   207\t\n   208\t```clojure\n   209\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.code\n   210\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   211\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   212\t :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/stack-free-names}\n   213\t```\n   214\t\n   215\tThe payload stored in Jing is the canonical instruction vector value itself,\n   216\tat its own `segment-key`.\n   217\t- `image-hash` is B1's hash over the descriptor hash and canonical vector.\n   218\t- `image-defect` validates opcodes, operands, bounds, body arity chains,\n   219\t  and host scalar support.\n   220\t- `stack-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning `:load-free`\n   221\t  operands in the canonical vector), preserving \"Existing edits: none\".\n   222\t- The lift to `:yin.code/*` is `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lift`.\n   223\t\n   224\t### 5.2 Register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`)\n   225\t\n   226\t```clojure\n   227\t{:format        :yin.debruijn.register\n   228\t :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   229\t :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   230\t :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/register-free-names}\n   231\t```\n   232\t\n   233\tThe payload stored in Jing is the register image map `{:bodies ...\n   234\t:instructions ...}`, at its own `segment-key`.\n   235\t- `register-hash` is R1's hash over the descriptor hash and register vector.\n   236\t- `register-image-defect` validates opcodes, register bounds, body ranges,\n   237\t  and the section 4.5 ascending, bounded, exact live sets.\n   238\t- `register-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning\n   239\t  `:load-free` operands across all bodies in the instruction vector),\n   240\t  preserving \"Existing edits: none\".\n   241\t- The lift to `:yin.code/*` is `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/lift`.\n   242\t\n   243\tBecause `fetch` is parameterized by this record, adding future VM formats\n   310\t   code itself is stored in `dao.jing`. DaoStream transports the requests and\n   311\t   the retrieved byte payloads; DaoJing content-addresses and stores the\n   312\t   underlying immutable data.\n   313\t3. **Parked requests**: A VM encountering an unlinked `:call-hash` (in B7)\n   314\t   parks its continuation and emits a request carrying the identity (H or R).\n   315\t   The composition resolves the identity through its index and initiates\n   316\t   `fetch`. All request tokens, continuations, and response outcomes are pure\n   317\t   data on the stream.\n   318\t\n   319\t## 10. File box: Phase B6\n   320\t\n   321\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   322\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n   323\t    Existing edits: none\n   324\t    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-matches?, segment-key, materialize!, get),\n   325\t                dao.jing.dht (create-content-dht, IDhtNet),\n   326\t                dao.jing.remote (content-client, default-handlers),\n   327\t                B1 (image-hash, image-defect, descriptor),\n   328\t                B2 (lift),\n   329\t                R1 (register-hash, register-image-defect, descriptor, lift)\n   330\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, register-hash,\n   331\t                     dao.stream, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote,\n   332\t                     named VM semantics\n   333\t\n   334\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` exports:\n   335\t- `fetch`: the parameterized fetch function.\n   336\t- `stack-format`: the `:yin.debruijn.code` format record.\n   337\t- `register-format`: the `:yin.debruijn.register` format record.\n   338\t- `stack-free-names`: scans free names from stack instruction vectors.\n   339\t- `register-free-names`: scans free names from register image maps.\n   340\t- `verify-same-root-pairing`: verifies an H and R pair against source datoms.\n   341\t- Qualified refusal constructors and predicates.\n   342\t\n   343\t## 11. Completion criteria: Phase B6\n   344\t\n   345\tCross-host transfer over `dao.stream`:\n   346\t\n   347\t1. (H, R) JVM to Dart transfer over `dao.jing.remote`'s DaoStream transport,\n   348\t   where the receiver initially knows only the identity (H or R) and an\n   349\t   index. Includes the Dart-side client harness for that path.\n   350\t2. (H, R) Equal normalized results under the B0 normalizer when the fetched\n   351\t   image is lifted and executed through the existing semantic VM, compared\n   352\t   to local execution.\n   353\t3. (H, R) The cross-host corpus is restricted to print-stable scalars until\n   354\t   CBOR lands; no golden test pins a Jing address.\n   355\t\n   356\tRefusals:\n   357\t\n   358\t4. (H, R) `:absent` for an identity the index cannot resolve.\n   359\t5. (H, R) `:address-mismatch` when the received value does not match the\n   360\t   address algorithm and digest, tested via:\n   361\t   a. Local storage corruption.\n   362\t   b. Corrupt client RPC response over DaoStream.\n   363\t6. (shared) A DHT peer serving mismatched content is rejected before load;\n   364\t   `make-get` filters invalid peer payloads, returning `:absent` if no peer\n   365\t   has valid data.\n   366\t7. (H, R) `:hash-mismatch` when a well-stored value at the indexed address\n   367\t   does not hash to the requested identity, including descriptor or contract\n   368\t   version disagreement.\n   369\t8. (H, R) Structural validator defect in a fetched image refused before load.\n   370\t9. (R) Live-set defect in a fetched register image refused before load.\n   371\t10. (H, R) `:unresolved-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver cannot\n   372\t    resolve through primitives or modules.\n   373\t11. (H, R) `:shadowed-free` for a `:load-free` name shadowed by the\n   374\t    receiver's free environment or store.\n   375\t12. (R) Trusted fallback from R to H that names its trust in the outcome.\n   376\t13. (R) Verifying fallback that re-lowers named datoms, refuses a swapped\n   377\t    pairing with `:pairing-mismatch`, and accepts an authentic pairing.\n   378\t\n   379\tStructure:\n   380\t\n   381\t14. One fetch function serves both stack and register formats without\n   382\t    format-specific branching in `fetch`.\n   383\t15. Tests use an explicit `fetch` call; no `:call-hash` instruction is\n   384\t    emitted or consumed (deferred to B7).\n   385\t16. No global loader, registry, callback, or cache is introduced; handle\n   386\t    and index are explicit arguments.\n   387\t17. Tri-host parity: JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart pass all tests cleanly,\n   388\t    with 0 kondo errors and clean cljstyle formatting.\n   389\t\n   390\t## 12. Non-goals\n   391\t\n   392\tThe B6 linker does not:\n   393\t\n   394\t- Add transport, peer lookup, caching, or responders (owned by DaoJing).\n   395\t- Emit or interpret `:call-hash` (deferred to B7).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
