{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '998,1080p'\nrg -n 'R5|debruijn_register_linker|built together|B6|register-free-names|stack-free-names' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md\"","aggregated_output":"   998\tdepends on R2, for the register shapes of stream, gensym, FFI, park, and\n   999\tresume, and on B4's engine seam, which supplies the restore and park-entry\n  1000\tconventions the kernel must share. Its completion requires parity for\n  1001\tvalues, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and blocked states, and\n  1002\tdeterministic stream/effect behavior. The pure-program tier may merge\n  1003\tbefore R2 lands; R4 is complete only when both tiers are.\n  1004\t\n  1005\tThe phase order runs on two parallel tracks after R1: on the register\n  1006\ttrack, R2's descriptor and liveness extension and R4's pure-program tier,\n  1007\tthen R4's effects tier once B4 and R2 exist, then R3 against the real\n  1008\tkernel; on the linker track, R5 together with B6, depending on R1 and B6\n  1009\tonly and free to land before R2 or R4.\n  1010\t\n  1011\t### R5: linker integration over dao.stream\n  1012\t\n  1013\t    Fulfilled by: Phase B6 (src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc,\n  1014\t    test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc)\n  1015\t    Specification: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md\n  1016\t    Must not change: stack H, dao.stream, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n  1017\t\n  1018\tPer the owner's directive (\"B6 should be used by both the stack and register\n  1019\tvm for linking code over dao.stream\"), the register format linking capabilities\n  1020\tare fulfilled directly within the unified B6 linker phase. Phase B6 delivers\n  1021\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc`, providing the parameterized `fetch`\n  1022\tfunction, the register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`), the R index,\n  1023\tand same-root pairing datoms (`[root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]`) with\n  1024\tboth trusted and verifying fallback paths. Full specification, file box,\n  1025\tand completion criteria are defined in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`.\n  1026\t\n  1027\t## 7. Non-goals and protected surfaces\n  1028\t\n  1029\tThis design does not alter any B0-B7 phase's semantics, the stack VM, named\n  1030\tdatoms, the dormant projection, or the AST-walker. It does not change B1's\n  1031\tH, its scalar bytes, its validator, or its wire protocol. It does not add\n  1032\tequality saturation, a JIT, lambda lifting, ANF, global distribution, or a\n  1033\tnew continuation interchange format. B2's adoption of the resolver is the\n  1034\tdecided shared upstream recorded in section 2.2.\n  1035\t\n  1036\tThe register lowerer consumes the resolved tuples, never the stack image and\n  1037\tnever named datoms directly. It is not a second stack lowerer: it emits only\n  1038\t`:yin.debruijn.register/*` and B2 remains the sole producer of\n  1039\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*`. Its input remains available after the transform.\n  1040\tRegister code is not portable to a stack-only host; such a host obtains a\n  1041\tstack image for the same program by H or by lowering the named datoms.\n  1042\t\n  1043\tThe resolver is not a second lossless source view. It has no identity, is\n  1044\tnot persisted, and is not a fetch key; its inverse exists for tests only.\n  1045\t\n  1046\t## 8. Risks and decisions\n  1047\t\n  1048\tRisks include register-allocation drift, spill policy becoming observable,\n  1049\teffect ordering, register continuation shape, drift between the stack and\n  1050\tregister lowerers, and the permanent second-evaluator maintenance surface\n  1051\taccepted in DECIDED 1.\n  1052\t\n  1053\tDECIDED:\n  1054\t\n  1055\t1. R0-R2 are lowerer and format work. R4, the register kernel, is\n  1056\t   authorized to proceed as soon as its prerequisites exist (R1 for the\n  1057\t   pure-program tier, R2 and B4's engine seam for the effects tier) and is\n  1058\t   not contingent on R3's report or any benchmark. This is a deliberate\n  1059\t   architectural commitment to a second evaluator, made by the owner on\n  1060\t   2026-09-23 after the tradeoff was explained: every future opcode, effect,\n  1061\t   or contract change is implemented and tested twice, in the stack kernel\n  1062\t   and the register kernel, across JVM, CLJS, and CLJD, permanently. The\n  1063\t   owner accepted that cost because the kernel is the end-to-end proof that\n  1064\t   the `dao.stream` compilation pipeline is genuinely configurable with\n  1065\t   swappable peer backends (section 1). The commitment is not informational\n  1066\t   and is not revisited by R3's numbers.\n  1067\t2. The stack image and the register image are peer projections of the\n  1068\t   resolved tuples. H and R are peer executable-format identities; neither\n  1069\t   preimage includes the other, and neither is a replacement semantic\n  1070\t   identity.\n  1071\t3. The resolved tuples are a derived stage value with a shape contract and a\n  1072\t   namespace but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role, per invariant I,\n  1073\t   D9, D12, and \"derive, do not persist\".\n  1074\t4. Lowering is a pure, non-destructive stream transform. Named datoms,\n  1075\t   resolved tuples, stack images, and register images coexist.\n  1076\t5. Lexical depth and position remain explicit. Locals and temporaries use\n  1077\t   disjoint register banks.\n  1078\t6. Allocation and any move order are canonicalized by evaluation and\n  1079\t   virtual-id order, with a versioned descriptor and golden fixtures.\n  1080\t7. The register kernel is a sibling and cannot silently replace the stack\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:1:# yin.vm de Bruijn linker over dao.stream (Phase B6)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:8:`dao.stream` (backed by `dao.jing` content addressing). It is Phase B6 of\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:11:Governing rule: **B6 is used by both the stack VM and the register VM for\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:35:Phase B6 is implemented alone as the dedicated linker phase. It establishes\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:55:B6 reuses this substrate wholesale. The linker writes no transport, no peer\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:58:### 2.1 What B6 adds\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:60:B6 contributes:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:72:### 2.2 What B6 does not add\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:204:B6 provides the format records for both execution backends:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:212: :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/stack-free-names}\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:220:- `stack-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning `:load-free`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:230: :free-names-fn yin.vm.debruijn-linker/register-free-names}\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:238:- `register-free-names` is defined by the linker module (scanning\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:265:trust, and provenance. Phase B6 requires only a plain data value.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:319:## 10. File box: Phase B6\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:338:- `stack-free-names`: scans free names from stack instruction vectors.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:339:- `register-free-names`: scans free names from register image maps.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:343:## 11. Completion criteria: Phase B6\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:392:The B6 linker does not:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:298:that B6's rule \"a fetched image may be executed by the semantic VM after\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:674:the R5 or B6 linker. This rule is deliberate; the alternative was a pair\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1008:kernel; on the linker track, R5 together with B6, depending on R1 and B6\n